    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Atelier / Maker's Mark",
    "overview": "Atelier is the default authenticated workspace: neutral surfaces, compact controls, a quiet navigation rail and fire-orange actions. The existing Maker's Mark supplies its signature. This records the implemented system alongside the supported Classic and Monolith choices; light/dark remains an independent setting.\n\nPublic sign-in is the owner-selected Editorial surface: white, plum ink, self-hosted Baskervville headings and system UI body. Its palette remains local and independent of saved app themes. The restrained form sits beside a charcoal Noveri demonstration; this composition belongs to its surface brief, not to every future screen.\n\nPerformance & Talent is the second scoped surface (D-P1-32 D3, APR-025, ADR-0152). Every page under `/performance`, employee and administration, renders inside `<div data-area-theme=\"performance\">` (app/performance/layout.tsx), and app/performance/area-theme.css re-declares the app's own tokens there: a navy band with the area title, white 12px cards on a light grey page, navy headings and primary, hairline rows, red for AI actions with the app's own sparkles icon, pills, segmented tabs and a stage rail (`pt-*` classes). The area looks the same in Classic, Monolith and Atelier: the `monolith:` and `atelier:` variants do not match inside it and the themes' structural rules are reverted there. It has its own navy-tinted dark variant; light/dark stays the user's setting. Control sizes follow the source on a fine pointer and keep 44px targets on a coarse one. Portaled overlays (dialogs, menus, toasts) render outside the wrapper and keep the user's theme. No logo or client name is drawn.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat shell and cards with hairline boundaries.",
      "Compact sentence-case operational hierarchy.",
      "Orange action/focus accent in Atelier; independent plum Editorial sign-in.",
      "Human-confirmation signature and semantic status labels."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Scoped Accent Rule",
        "body": "Use Atelier orange for actions, focus and small emphasis; keep public sign-in plum inside its own surface.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Surface Rule",
        "body": "Keep the shell and cards flat; use the existing small control shadow, ask-box lift and overlay shadow for their established roles.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do reuse the shared card, field, button and table hooks so Atelier styling reaches new operational screens.",
      "Do keep status text alongside its color and preserve tabular figures in tables.",
      "Do preserve supported saved theme choices; Atelier supplies the fallback.",
      "Do retain visible keyboard focus and the reduced-motion path."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't apply the public login split or plum palette to authenticated screens by default.",
      "Don't replace the existing Atelier identity or restyle Classic and Monolith through unscoped overrides.",
      "Don't treat the presence of a dark palette as a completed contrast audit."
    ]
  }
}
